over fruit quality  and multiply all that by four to five  year juvenile cycle per  um per round of uh crossing if you were  trying to do  multiple iterations of a back crossing  scheme to a current parent  that can take quite a long time is  infeasible for a lot of  a lot of breeders that just focus on  fruit quality primarily  and on top of that you have  incompatibility where miles domestica  can only really cross with uh the three  main  progenitor species uh as opposed to the  50 or 60  other species that exist um and when  we're breeding for resistance we're  looking  primarily at these mbs llr genes  that are strong triggers and detectors  of the pathogen as well as  elucidators of a strong effect or  triggered immunity in host defense  pathways  so the most characterized one we have is  a mr5 that comes from mal's robusta  and it's a coil coil mbsl uh mbslr gene  uh and with that is is already found to  have strains that overcome that  resistance so  even if you were to accomplish that goal  of breeding resistance into an elite  cultivar  uh with that comes an added selection  pressure to accelerate the  pathogen adaptation and um  ultimately go through this boom and bust  cycle where you can have multiple years  of strong resistance  and then a breakdown in a volatile next  couple of years  and this is where i think uh  susceptibility genes as a breeding  target can really fill in some of those  gaps so we classify it more as a  recessive resistance  given it's it's described better as a  loss of function mutation uh  and we can classify this in certain ways  it's a very broad  uh set of terms instead of targets so  anything that allows for pathogen  compatibility you're facilitating host  assistance going from  a host to a non-host or  encoding negative regulators of immune  signaling  that pathogen can leverage and turn off  immune signaling  and any of those regulatory elements as  well as a sustained compatibility this  could be over multiple years of disease  infection  where the plant could have a certain  structure or  target that fulfills metabolic or  structural needs like a sugar  transporter that is  hijacked by the pathogen and left in an  active conformation  so two quick examples from literature  and then other crops is  cslob1 which is uh done in grapefruit  with conservative citrus  um it's a lateral organ boundaries  domain transcription factor  that is targeted by xanthomonas citri  which is a bacterial disease  and what they found is the knockout of  this gene uh reduced susceptibility with  minimal fitness costs which is one of  the biggest criticisms of susceptibility  genes  um and with os sweet which was found in  rice  and knocked out kind of my example  earlier you have the sugar transporter  that is targeted by santa monica species  from these transcription activator like  effectors  and with the knockout of this gene again  you have reduced susceptibility  and they found no no fitness cost  statistically  so looking at uh my specific genes  in apple they're more disease-specific  so you have hrpn interacting protein of  malice  and you have dsp a slash e interacting  protein and malice hypomandipum  both are transmembrane proteins and both  interact with  their own respective erwenia affected  proteins  and the main goal of the the vector  proteins is to try to  stop any kind of immune signaling well  the main purpose of these transmembrane  proteins  is to try to capture any  signals um early signals of  extracellular  uh molecular patterns of erwinia um to  try to  elucidate out an early defense response  uh well the effector protein tries to  try to block that and inhibit any kind  of downstream pathways  so uh this is since these genes are  conserved across  malice uh we're interested to see what  the genetic variation is across  a diverse set of genotypes so across 93  genotypes in the germplasm inoculated  them all with  fire blight in the greenhouse and  measured their  their lesion length in the chute and in  the leaf and across the total distance  um  and then generated amplicon sequencing  data for each of the eight genes  and with the alignments identified 87  total snip variants  and performed marker trait association  uh just straightforward  wallace each test for each of the snips  you can see on the manhattan plot on the  left  there's a few there's 30 totally unique  snips that had significant effect on the  tree  but two that we highlighted in diaphragm  2b that was found in 2018  2019 um as well as in multiple tissue  types and  multiple traits and diaphragm 4b which  was found in  higher significance for three traits um  and you can see  across multiple years there's a  significant difference in the phenotype  based on the snip class  i'm also interested to see if there's an  additive response to having  a combination of the lower  susceptibility snips and higher  susceptibility steps you can see the  first two columns here  in the box plot this is a combination of  genotype that has  both reduced susceptibility snips and  both increase susceptibility snips  showing the most extreme classes with  intermediate responses for the mixture  um the mixture of the two classes  we also looked at the distribution of  these snips  across um the different species  wild and domesticated that we use in the  study  with the you know basic conclusions  coming out as  the vast majority of males domestica  sessions had increased susceptibility  snips with a few notable  interesting accession exceptions of some  interesting cultivars  and the wild had a mixture of both  susceptible and  increased and decreased susceptibility  that were likely  masked by the resistances that  already exist within those genotypes  so even i were to make really specific  markers uh out of that data  it still comes with a lot of issues that  tree breeding brings so genome editing  is a really promising technique  um to knock out susceptibility genes and  uh produce really nice pre-breeding  lines  or elite cultivars with heritable  mutations  so you could do this via biological  vector with agrobacterium  and have integration of tdna into the  genome  and perform the double strand break  non-homologous end joining  and get that early stop code and then  knocks out the gene but what this ends  up happening is  you have a  regulation you have a  a certain level of regulation that you  have to follow and segregating out that  vector backbone is is not  feasible in tree crops given the long  tube in that cycle  so what i'm working on is a dna free  technique  um using biologic bombardment or  protoplast  where you introduce free and stable  sgrnas and cas9 to perform the same task  but in a dna free manner which is lower  on the regulations and you still get  heritable mutation  and elite cultivar while skipping at  least one reproductive cycle  and this is just some of my work here  i'm targeting diaphragm 4a  hyphen 1b and the genotypes gallon  honeycrisp  via bombardment agrobacterium with an  interest in typing 2b which was from  the previous work i presented and  uh just some validation uh from previous  literature  you can see a knockdown of hyphen 1b  and see the wild type here of galaxy the  cultivar galaxy is very susceptible  and the transgenic explants are  having quite a bit of produced  susceptibility and  more recently you have a christopher  knockout full knockout of  diaper 4a in gala and golden delicious  where you have  susceptible response for both and then  the transgenic plants  have reduced susceptibility and  significant upper growth  which is a good sign so part of my the  interest of my work is to do this in a  dna free manner  test this in different genetic  backgrounds and with  against a diversity of different strains  of bacteria  and to to really test the robustness  possibly with even double mutants of  these genes to see if there's an  additivity that holds up  um similar to the data i presented  earlier  so with that i'd like to thank dr vascon  um some of our lab members giuliani and  della  as well as my funding from ardp and some  of the collaborators on the  genetic variation project um michaela  monoi  diego micheletti and dr villarreal  philly  and with that i'd be happy to take any  questions  anytime i have that  thank you for the wonderful talk ricky  that's really good  um yeah you just you were perfect in  time so we have five minutes for  discussion awesome yeah i am monitoring  the chat for you  yeah i can see the chat so okay  um yeah let me read the question out  loud um  so there's one question from ed he's  saying cool an editing process how did  you control for population structure in  the associate  associations yeah so  um we tested for uh we ran a pca  and noticed uh there wasn't too much  population structure  uh and when utilizing that in the model  given us a small data set there seemed  to be some overfitting in the model so  these were just kind of straightforward  um  wallace park assistant uh arbitrate  associations  um can i go ahead this is jean-luc  um so you talked about these  susceptibility genes that when you knock  them out they  gain resistance without any detrimental  side effect but  like why would a why would a species or  a plant want to keep those around  and i mean i think it's totally possible  maybe they come through  you know they are generally a  deleterious  and you know drifts can bring  deleterious genes to fixation and so on  but  otherwise say there was some beneficial  effect  that you just haven't noticed what would  what would your lead hypothesis be  on the the value of these of these  alleles  yeah so the contribution of keeping the  susceptibility alleles within the plant  is actually really vital given that  a lot of them are associated with  certain basic plant functions  so um in the case of the sugar  transporter that's that's something that  the plant be you know in a regular case  would  would want to 